CAPTURE INSTRUMENT, no graded answer. It evacuates two tanks off the west edge and
photographs the refund ticks they leave behind. It asserts nothing about whether the
ticks rendered, because nothing it can call CAN see that: no Lua binding reports a live
FloatingText, and the two things that were fixed -- the Map.Bounds clamp on the spawn
position and FloatingText's ignoreVisibility bypass -- are both purely presentational
and reach the screen without passing through any state a script can read.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. The Lua evaluates no predicate about the mod. Its runtime checks
are guards on the INSTRUMENT: that World.RenderPlayer is live (via a never-scouted
pillbox's mouse-targetability), that both subjects actually sold and how much the engine
credited, that the two sales landed close enough together for both ticks to still be
alive in one frame, and that the Payload: 0 override really produced a zero. Every one
of those can only downgrade the frame to "not evidence". None can conclude that the
indicator works. A Test.Pass would contribute a green to every run-batch.sh --all tally
whether or not a single pixel of text rendered.

The parts that ARE gradeable are graded elsewhere and nothing here should duplicate
them: OpenRA.Test/EvacRefundTextMathTest.cs pins ClampToBounds and the lifetime/rise
arithmetic.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever gives the scenario a real assertion. The
obvious candidate is a Lua binding that reports the world's live FloatingText effects --
their text, position and remaining lifetime -- which would let the run assert that a tick
carrying "+$0" exists at the clamped boundary cell instead of asking a human to read four
glyphs off a PNG. Test.GetImpactEffectCount (TestGlobal.cs:1143) is the precedent for
counting effects from Lua, so the shape is established; it was deliberately not added
blind because the useful version needs position and text, not a count.

A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades RED on purpose, so a scenario that starts
grading announces itself instead of silently changing meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail`
declarations, where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green.
A timeout reaches expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED.
